  <!-- PART 2: SOUND DATASETS -->
  <h1>Part 2: Bioacoustics & Passive Acoustic Monitoring (Sound Datasets)</h1>

  <!-- PROBLEM 3 -->
  <h2>Problem 3: Polyphonic Bird Song Identification in Dense Multi-Species Soundscapes</h2>
  <span class="badge">Primary Dataset: BirdCLEF (Kaggle)</span>
  <span class="badge">Modality: Bioacoustics, Audio Spectrogram Classification</span>

  <div class="callout-problem">
    <strong>Conservation Problem Statement:</strong><br>
    Bird species serve as fundamental indicator species for ecological health and forest regeneration. However, avian monitoring via human observers in dense canopies (e.g., the Amazon, Western Ghats, or Sundarbans) is physically constrained and cost-prohibitive. Automated continuous audio recorders capture terabytes of dense soundscapes daily. In these recordings, dozens of bird species sing simultaneously (polyphony) against loud ambient noise (wind, heavy rain, insect stridulation, rivers). Identifying quiet, critically endangered species hidden beneath dominant songbirds is a massive computational bottleneck.
  </div>

  <div class="callout-solution">
    <strong>Proposed Technical Solution:</strong><br>
    Transform continuous raw audio streams into high-resolution <strong>Log-Mel Spectrograms</strong> and feed them into a <strong>Sound Event Detection (SED) architecture</strong> using a <strong>Pretrained Audio Spectrogram Transformer (AST) / ResNet-34 with Attention Pooling</strong>. The model handles multi-label outputs (sigmoid activations per species) and outputs fine-grained time-stamped probability curves for every species singing in the 5-second window.
  </div>

  <h3>End-to-End Audio Pipeline Architecture</h3>
  <table class="data-table">
    <tr>
      <th>Stage</th>
      <th>Technical Operation</th>
      <th>Output Specification</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>1. Audio Resampling</td>
      <td>Resample audio clips to uniform sample rate (32,000 Hz); 5-second chunking.</td>
      <td>Mono waveform, $1 \times 160,000$ samples</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>2. Time-Frequency Transform</td>
      <td>Short-Time Fourier Transform (STFT) &rarr; Log-Mel Filterbank ($N_{mels} = 128$, $f_{min}=50\text{ Hz}$, $f_{max}=14,000\text{ Hz}$).</td>
      <td>2D Spectrogram tensor $(128 \times 313)$</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>3. Data Augmentation</td>
      <td>SpecAugment (Frequency masking & Time masking) + Background noise mixup.</td>
      <td>Robust augmented spectrogram</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>4. Neural Classifier</td>
      <td>ResNet-34 backbone with Multi-Head Attention Pooling & Sigmoid layer.</td>
      <td>Multi-label species probabilities $[0, 1]^K$</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h3>Python Implementation Code (Audio Feature Extraction & Model)</h3>
  <pre><code>import torch
import torch.nn as nn
import torchaudio.transforms as T
import torchaudio

class BioacousticBirdClassifier(nn.Module):
    def __init__(self, num_species=264, sample_rate=32000, n_mels=128):
        super(BioacousticBirdClassifier, self).__init__()
        # Audio to Log-Mel Spectrogram conversion on GPU
        self.mel_spectrogram = T.MelSpectrogram(
            sample_rate=sample_rate,
            n_fft=2048,
            win_length=2048,
            hop_length=512,
            n_mels=n_mels,
            f_min=150.0,
            f_max=15000.0
        )
        self.amplitude_to_db = T.AmplitudeToDB()
        
        # Convolutional Feature Extractor
        self.conv_block = nn.Sequential(
            nn.Conv2d(1, 32, kernel_size=3, padding=1),
            nn.BatchNorm2d(32),
            nn.ReLU(),
            nn.MaxPool2d(2),
            nn.Conv2d(32, 64, kernel_size=3, padding=1),
            nn.BatchNorm2d(64),
            nn.ReLU(),
            nn.MaxPool2d(2),
            nn.AdaptiveAvgPool2d((1, 1))
        )
        # Multi-label prediction head (polyphonic detection)
        self.classifier = nn.Linear(64, num_species)
        self.sigmoid = nn.Sigmoid()

    def forward(self, waveform):
        # waveform: [Batch, Samples] -> [Batch, 1, n_mels, time]
        mel = self.mel_spectrogram(waveform).unsqueeze(1)
        log_mel = self.amplitude_to_db(mel)
        features = self.conv_block(log_mel).squeeze(-1).squeeze(-1)
        logits = self.classifier(features)
        probs = self.sigmoid(logits)
        return probs

# Example execution
dummy_audio = torch.randn(4, 160000) # 4 audio clips, 5 seconds at 32kHz
detector = BioacousticBirdClassifier(num_species=152)
predictions = detector(dummy_audio)
print(f"Batch prediction matrix shape: {predictions.shape} (Batches x Species)")
</code></pre>

  <p><strong>Evaluation & Field Impact:</strong> Evaluated using <strong>class-macro average ROC-AUC (cmAP)</strong>. Enables automated biodiversity auditing for large protected national parks, reducing processing latency from 6 months of manual ornithologist review to under 2 hours of GPU processing.</p>

